Ball physics: move in substeps to avoid tunneling: move in steps of at most r/2.

Collision with blocks: check AABB vs circle approximation — simpler: treat ball as a point expanded by r, and determine which side based on penetration depth.

Standard approach:

```js
function ballBlockCollision(b, blk){
  // closest point
  const cx = Math.max(blk.x, Math.min(b.x, blk.x+blk.w));
  const cy = Math.max(blk.y, Math.min(b.y, blk.y+blk.h));
  const dx = b.x-cx, dy=b.y-cy;
  if (dx*dx+dy*dy <= b.r*b.r) {
    // determine bounce direction by comparing center overlap
    const ox = (b.x - (blk.x+blk.w/2)) / (blk.w/2 + b.r);
    const oy = (b.y - (blk.y+blk.h/2)) / (blk.h/2 + b.r);
    if (Math.abs(ox) > Math.abs(oy)) b.dx = -b.dx; else b.dy = -b.dy;
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

Only one bounce per block per frame; break after collision to avoid weirdness? Better to handle one collision per substep.

Substep loop: steps = Math.ceil(speed / (r/2)).

Paddle collision: if ball is moving downward and ball.y + r >= paddle.y && ball.y + r <= paddle.y + paddle.h + speed && ball.x >= paddle.x - r && ball.x <= paddle.x + paddle.w + r → reflect.

relative = (ball.x - (paddle.x + paddle.w/2)) / (paddle.w/2), clamp to [-1,1]. angle = relative * 60°. dx = speed*sin(angle), dy = -speed*cos(angle).

Walls: left/right/top reflect. Bottom: lose a life.

